--- MESSAGE ---
++ is the first operator the drake parser trees, and a substitution string is its own form

Dev's shape for the first transform type put the design question on the table: a substitution
string is "string literals interleaved with function call literals", and the corpus's only spelling
of that today is Scala's s"…" interpolation, carried opaque. Drake needs its own, applicable to every
target. Juxtaposition — Dev's first draft — is already application in the grammar drake adopted, so
`packagePart package " "` would read as a two-argument call; the literal cannot rescue it without
deciding by token type, which the spec's own rule (close the grammar, don't tie-break it) forbids.
Haskell has the operator for exactly this interleaving, and it keeps the shape intact:

    fix value String "domain " ++ [ packagePart parameters par package ] ++ " " ++ name

THE TREE. {"++": [pieces…]} — flat and associative like ".", one node holding every piece in order.
A leaf piece is a literal or a name; an application piece is an ordinary "()" node.

THE SURFACE. ` ++ ` between pieces. An application piece BRACKETS ITSELF, for the reason a nested
argument does: its `par` list cannot bound itself, and the `]` is what ends it before the next `++`.
It follows that a `++` arriving after an argument belongs to the argument, by the nearest-open-
construct rule that gives a `.member` to the pending chain; to concatenate onto the call, bracket
the call. `++` is a reserved token — that is the whole parser change in principle: a value slot
stops at it as at any keyword, and the operand before it is closed with no layout consulted.

THE ENGINE. ScalaTarget renders `++` as `++`, not `+`: Scala's `++` on a String demands a String
operand, where `+` would coerce anything to text — the tighter one is the one that catches a slot
fed the wrong type. And defaultInitializer, which quotes any tree in a String-typed slot (the limit
6f5a8bb found, which kept Emission's body opaque), now exempts a concatenation: a `++` tree is a
COMPUTATION of the string, not its surface text. That exemption is what lets the next commit author
DomainLine's value as a tree at all.

WHAT IS NOT HERE. A definition using it. The corpus carries no `++`, so the gates exercise the
new code only through the reserved set; the one structural test in DrakeParseTest is the whole
exercise — tree shape, surface round-trip, JSON round-trip, engine rendering, on exactly the
DomainLine shape. The lesson from the case increment stands: an increment that authors nothing
cannot exercise the engine, and the next commit authors.

